<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Data Canon</title><link>https://marianettigroup.github.io/data-canon/</link><description>Recent content on Data Canon</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><atom:link href="https://marianettigroup.github.io/data-canon/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Augmented Parsing</title><link>https://marianettigroup.github.io/data-canon/docs/guides/augmented-parsing/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://marianettigroup.github.io/data-canon/docs/guides/augmented-parsing/</guid><description>&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="overview"&gt;Overview&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Canonical DataCanon is intentionally minimal. It contains no variables,&#10;expression evaluation, or general-purpose computation.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;For human-authored input, however, a small number of conveniences can make&#10;structured scientific data substantially easier to enter.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;augmented_parse()&lt;/code&gt; provides these conveniences without extending the canonical&#10;DataCanon language. Augmented syntax is transformed into ordinary DataCanon&#10;before being passed to the canonical parser.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="background-color:#f8f8f8;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-text" data-lang="text"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Augmented input&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; ↓&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;List Shorthand&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; ↓&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;General substitutions&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; global → local&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; ↓&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Mathematical substitutions&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; ↓&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Canonical parser&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; ↓&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Data Skeleton&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Like &lt;code&gt;parse()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;augmented_parse()&lt;/code&gt; always returns a Data Skeleton.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>